  "translation": {
    "binders": ["q is an arbitrary probability mass function on the real numbers."],
    "hypotheses": [],
    "conclusions": ["The pushforward of q under the affine map x ↦ 2x - 1 equals the probability mass function placing probability 1/2 at each of -1 and 1 if and only if q equals the probability mass function placing probability 1/2 at each of 0 and 1."],
    "mathematical_definitions": [
      "Let T : ℝ → ℝ be T(x) = 2x - 1.",
      "Let B be a fair Boolean random variable, with probability 1/2 for true and probability 1/2 for false.",
      "The {0,1}-valued reference law is the pushforward of B under true ↦ 1 and false ↦ 0.",
      "The {-1,1}-valued reference law is the pushforward of B under true ↦ 1 and false ↦ -1.",
      "PMF.map denotes pushforward of a probability mass function."
    ],
    "proposition_plain_english": "For every real-valued probability mass function q, transforming a variable with law q by x ↦ 2x - 1 produces the symmetric law on {-1,1} exactly when q is the fair law on {0,1}."
  },
  "restrictions_and_vacuity_risks": [
    "The statement concerns probability mass functions, hence discrete laws, rather than arbitrary probability measures on ℝ.",
    "All distributional comparisons are exact equalities of probability mass functions; no approximate, almost-everywhere, moment, or convergence notion is present.",
    "No support, integrability, independence, or moment assumptions on q are encoded.",
    "The forward implication relies on the injectivity of x ↦ 2x - 1 on ℝ; because this map is injective, no additional input laws can collapse to the same pushforward.",
    "The Boolean convention is explicit: true maps to 1 in both encodings, while false maps to 0 for the input reference law and to -1 for the output reference law.",
    "The proofs that 2 is a valid numeral and that 1/2 ≤ 1 are construction-time certificates, not mathematical hypotheses on q.",
    "There is no evident vacuity: both reference probability mass functions are well-defined, and the fair {0,1} law satisfies the asserted pushforward equality."
  ],
  "ambiguities": []
}
